// `#[compiler_resolved]` is refused where it would mean nothing, as `#[at_least_one]` and
// `#[effectful]` are. The attribute's only effect is to exempt a declaration from the rule that
// two of them may share a name only when every call can tell them apart -- so wearing it without
// needing it disables a live check and buys nothing.
//
// `one` has no sibling at all, which is the extreme case: there is nothing to be told apart from.
// A distinguishable pair is refused for the same reason, and the check runs over every declaration
// rather than only the second and later ones, which is why this one-declaration file reports.
#[compiler_resolved]
fn one(n: int) -> int {
    return n
}

fn main() {
    print(one(1))
}
